
mapWithDefault :
---------------
used to provide a default value when a source element is missing or null in the input message


Explanation:

Purpose:
The primary purpose of MapWithDefault is to handle scenarios where a source field is expected but
might be absent. It allows you to define a default value that will be used if the source field is not found in the input.

How it works:
When a mapping is executed, the MapWithDefault function checks if the source element exists.
If the source element is present, its value is passed to the target field. If the source element
is missing, the default value specified in the function's properties is used instead.

Use Cases:
Mandatory fields:
If a target field is mandatory but the corresponding source field might be missing,
MapWithDefault can be used to provide a default value to avoid errors.

Optional fields:
If a source field is optional and its presence is not guaranteed, MapWithDefault can be used to
assign a default value when the field is absent.

Handling null values: When the source field exists but its value is null, MapWithDefault can be
configured to replace the null value with a default value.

Example:
If the source field "ProductCategory" is mapped to the target field "Category" using MapWithDefault with
a default value of "General", then:

If "ProductCategory" exists and has a value (e.g., "Electronics"), "Electronics" will be mapped to "Category".
If "ProductCategory" is missing or has a null value, "General" will be mapped to "Category".

Node Functions:
MapWithDefault is a node function, meaning it operates on nodes (elements) within the message structure.

Alternatives:
While MapWithDefault is useful for providing default values, other functions like ReplaceValue can
be used for different scenarios, such as replacing empty values.